From f238b2d9aeb73c1fbc9dd32280054a58f836fd4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Swiftly <accounts@paulherter.de>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 18:14:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] input_clock: raise CR_MAX_GAP back to 60 seconds

The 300 ms threshold treats every timestamp gap in a pace-controlled source
as a stream discontinuity, because system_diff is near zero when the demuxer
reads faster than realtime. Measured on tvOS: 5276 clock resets in 72 s on a
file whose PCR interval is 0.3 to 1.0 s, until the app was killed. The same
file plays fine on VLC 3, which used 60 s.
---
 src/clock/input_clock.c | 33 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/clock/input_clock.c b/src/clock/input_clock.c
index 01d873b..6931660 100644
--- a/src/clock/input_clock.c
+++ b/src/clock/input_clock.c
@@ -83,7 +83,38 @@
  * my dice --Meuuh */
 #define CR_MEAN_PTS_GAP VLC_TICK_FROM_MS(300)
 
-#define CR_MAX_GAP CR_MEAN_PTS_GAP
+/* Swiftly: back to the pre-4.0 value.
+ *
+ * This used to be 60 seconds. "input_clock: finer discontinuity handling"
+ * lowered it to CR_MEAN_PTS_GAP (300 ms) together with the much better
+ * comparison a few lines below: a large stream_diff is fine as long as
+ * system_diff grew by the same amount.
+ *
+ * That comparison only holds when the source runs at its own pace. When we
+ * control the pace -- a plain file over HTTP -- the demuxer feeds PCRs as
+ * fast as it can read, so system_diff is essentially zero and every gap in
+ * the stream timestamps is larger than the threshold. The clock then resets
+ * its reference on every update.
+ *
+ * Measured on an Apple TV, one episode on a Jellyfin server, VLCKit 4.0.0-a23:
+ *
+ *     clock gap, unexpected stream discontinuity:
+ *         system_diff: 34   stream_diff: 320666
+ *
+ * 5276 of those in 72 seconds, 35 to 70 new clock contexts per second, until
+ * tvOS killed the app for burning the CPU. stream_diff over 2638 samples:
+ * min 303 ms, median 490 ms, max 1001 ms -- every single one just past the
+ * 300 ms threshold. Six other files on the same server, played for hours,
+ * produced none: this file simply carries sparse timestamps.
+ *
+ * The same file plays fine in VLC 3 (via another Jellyfin client on the same
+ * Apple TV), which is what pointed at the threshold in the first place.
+ *
+ * Raising it keeps the improved comparison and restores the tolerance that
+ * made VLC 3 cope. Streams that really are discontinuous still go backwards,
+ * and stream_diff < 0 catches that regardless of this value.
+ */
+#define CR_MAX_GAP VLC_TICK_FROM_SEC(60)
 
 /* Rate (in 1/256) at which we will read faster to try to increase our
  * internal buffer (if we control the pace of the source).
-- 
